      "criterion": "Given a props interface or type alias containing a computed key or a symbol-typed key (e.g. `interface Props { [key: string]: unknown; [Symbol.iterator]?: () => void; }`), those specific keys are omitted from the extracted prop list for that type \u2014 no diagnostic is emitted for the omission, and any other statically-named keys on the same interface/type are still extracted normally. A numeric-literal key (e.g. `0: string;`) is NOT omitted: it is extracted normally, with its decimal string form used as the prop name (the key `0` is extracted as prop name `\"0\"`).",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-019",
      "criterion": "Given a .tsx (or .ts) file with zero bytes of content, the extractor completes without error, producing a SourceData whose component_mappings, interfaces, type_aliases, enums, imports, exports, and diagnostics collections are all empty \u2014 an empty file is a valid input, not an error condition, and no diagnostic of any kind is emitted for it. As with AC-026, this criterion's emptiness assertion covers exactly those seven named fields; SourceData's remaining fields (type_alias_params, interface_type_params, const_arrays, and any param_defaults) are likewise empty for an empty file but are not independently asserted by any criterion in this spec.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-020",
      "criterion": "For any input file processed by parse_file(), no reference borrowed from the OXC allocator/AST arena used during that call is retained in, or reachable from, the returned SourceData or any Diagnostic/ComponentMapping it contains \u2014 every string, name, and type reference in the returned SourceData is independently owned (e.g. String/CompactString) rather than a borrow tied to the arena's lifetime, so the arena can be dropped immediately after parse_file() returns without invalidating anything the caller holds.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-021",
      "criterion": "Given a .tsx file containing N (N >= 2) distinct PascalCase bindings that each independently alias (per AC-003 or AC-005) the same already-collected base component declared lexically earlier in the file \u2014 e.g. `function Base(props: BaseProps) {} const A = Base; const Bcopy = Base;` \u2014 the SourceData ultimately returned by parse_file() contains a separate ComponentMapping entry for each of the N aliasing bindings, each carrying the base component's props type name (e.g. both `A` and `Bcopy` map to `BaseProps`), and the base component's own entry (`Base`) is absent from that SourceData \u2014 absent a qualifying `displayName` reassignment (position in the file relative to this declaration does not matter, per AC-027) targeting one of the N aliasing bindings, per AC-027, which supersedes that entry's key. No diagnostic is emitted for this case.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-022",
      "criterion": "Given a source file with a RECOVERABLE parse error \u2014 one or more syntax errors that the parser reports without setting its panicked/fatal state, e.g. `type Bad = Partial<>; function Ok(props: OkProps) { return null; }` \u2014 SourceData.diagnostics contains one diagnostic with code ParseError and Error severity per parser error encountered, each identifying the file path, AND every other declaration in the file that parsed cleanly is extracted exactly as it would be in an error-free file (e.g. `Ok`'s ComponentMapping entry, `Ok=>OkProps`, is still present).",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-023",
      "criterion": "During the extractor's own recursive type-collection pass over a single top-level type annotation (independent of AC-012's pre-parse bracket-nesting guard, which operates on raw source bytes before parsing, has its own threshold, and produces a different diagnostic code and severity), a per-annotation nesting counter tracks descent depth and resets at the start of each new top-level type annotation (not cumulative across the file); once that counter exceeds a fixed threshold of 200 (MAX_TYPE_COLLECT_DEPTH) for a given node, the extractor stops descending into that node's subtree entirely and represents the subtree using its verbatim source text rather than a structured type breakdown, and a Warning-severity diagnostic with code MaxDepthExceeded (identifying the file path) is emitted for that node. For a SINGLE-CHILD nesting chain (each level has exactly one nested TSType descendant \u2014 e.g. `A<A<A<...>>>` via TSTypeReference's one type argument, `{ a: { a: { a: ... } } }` via nested TSTypeLiteral/property types, or TSArrayType/TSParenthesizedType chains), exactly ONE diagnostic is emitted for the whole chain, since only the first node to cross the threshold is visited before descent stops \u2014 a chain of 250 and a chain of 400 single-child-nested nodes both produce exactly one MaxDepthExceeded diagnostic apiece. This single-diagnostic guarantee does NOT extend to MULTI-CHILD TSType forms: a TSConditionalType node (`T extends U ? X : Y`) descends into up to four children (check_type, extends_type, true_type, false_type) simultaneously at the same depth, so a chained-conditional-type nesting form that crosses the threshold at its boundary level emits one diagnostic per child branch that individually crosses the threshold there \u2014 up to 4 for a single boundary-level TSConditionalType node, not 1. The rest of the type annotation outside any truncated subtree, and extraction of the rest of the enclosing file, both continue normally in every case \u2014 this guard does not abandon the file the way AC-012's guard does. The counter's exact starting offset and increment rule per distinct entry path (a property's own top-level type vs. a type argument inside a generic reference vs. other recursion entry points) is an internal implementation detail not asserted by this criterion \u2014 different entry paths may trip the guard at slightly different chain depths near the 200 threshold; see non_goals.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-024",
      "criterion": "Given any of the following shapes where a props-type position that this spec otherwise requires to be a named-type reference is instead a union type, an intersection type, or an inline object-literal type \u2014 (a) a PascalCase-named function declaration's first parameter (AC-001's function-declaration path, e.g. `function Card(props: { title: string }) { return null; }` or `function Card(props: A | B) { return null; }`); (b) AC-001's own FC-family-annotation first type argument (AC-001's case (b) path, e.g. `const Card: React.FC<{ title: string }> = (props) => null;`) and AC-025's no-initializer equivalent of the same annotation path (e.g. `declare const Widget: React.FC<{ title: string }>;`) \u2014 both routed through the same shared type-name-extraction function as (a); (c) an inline function/arrow first-argument's first parameter inside a call-expression wrap (AC-006's shape, e.g. `const Wrapped = withStyles((props: { title: string }) => null)`); (d) a nested-forwardRef call's second type argument inside a call-expression wrap (AC-007's shape); or (e) a `forwardRef`/`React.forwardRef` call's own second type argument (AC-002's shape, e.g. `React.forwardRef<HTMLDivElement, { title: string }>(...)`) \u2014 SourceData.component_mappings contains an entry for that declaration whose props type name is a synthesized placeholder of the form `__anon_N` (N is a non-negative integer, unique within the file), AND SourceData.type_aliases contains an entry keyed by the file-scoped form `${file_path}:__anon_N` (matching the same `path:Name` scoping convention used for every other entry in SourceData.type_aliases) representing the union/intersection/object-literal structure. No diagnostic is emitted for this case. (AC-003 and AC-005's identifier-alias shapes have no parameter or type-argument position of their own to synthesize from \u2014 they inherit whatever props type name, anonymous or named, the referenced entry already carries \u2014 so this criterion does not apply to them directly.)",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-025",
      "criterion": "Given a `.tsx` or `.d.ts` file containing a PascalCase variable declared with no initializer whose type annotation is one of AC-001's six FC-family annotation names (`FC`, `FunctionComponent`, `ComponentType`, `VFC`, `VoidFunctionComponent`, `ForwardRefComponent`, each with or without a leading `React.` prefix) supplying a first type argument that is a direct named-type reference \u2014 e.g. `declare const Widget: React.FC<WidgetProps>;` \u2014 SourceData.component_mappings contains an entry for `Widget` whose props type name is `WidgetProps`. This pattern is gated by file extension identically to AC-001, AC-002, AC-003, AC-005, AC-006, and AC-007 (`.tsx` and `.d.ts` on, plain `.ts` off): the same annotation on the same no-initializer declaration in a plain `.ts` file produces NO ComponentMapping entry, because `try_fc_annotation` is only reachable when the file's source type is JSX or a TypeScript declaration file. AC-004 (the `ForwardRefExoticComponent` no-init shape) remains the sole pattern in this spec that is NOT gated by file extension \u2014 see AC-008.",
